<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ionia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ionian Greek, Ionian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ionia was the central part of the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, settled by Ionian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and home to cities such as Miletus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a> in 546 BCE, the Ionian cities came under Achaemenid rule. Their later revolt helped spark the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ionia.jpg" alt="Ionia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Athens2004 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ionia was an ancient region on the central part of the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, never a unified state. Ionia proper was a narrow coastal strip from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a> in the north to Miletus in the south, and included the islands of Chios and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, and it was bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolia</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> to the east and Caria to the south. Three fertile river valleys gave it a reputation as the most fertile region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. A core of twelve cities formed the Ionian League, with a shared sanctuary and festival at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panionium">Panionion</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a>. The League was mainly religious and cultural. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> generally identified themselves by their city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales of Miletus</a>'s advice to form a political union was rejected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> believed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> were descendants of Ion who left the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and then sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> under the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codrus">Codrus</a>. Greek settlement of Ionia probably took place in the 11th century BCE, though the lack of contemporary sources leaves the sequence unclear. In the Archaic period its cities were among the cultural, intellectual and political leaders of the Greek world, and Miletus founded colonies around the Euxine Sea.</p><p>The cities fell fully under Lydian rule under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> defeated Croesus they came under the Persian monarchy, with considerable autonomy under local tyrants loyal to the Persian king. About 500 BCE Histiaeus of Miletus's circle ignited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>, with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria; the rebels burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, but the Ionian fleet was beaten off Lade and Miletus was destroyed after a long siege. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale">Battle of Mycale</a> in 479 BCE secured their freedom, and they became dependent allies of Athens. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">Peace of Antalcidas</a> of 387 BCE recognised Persian possession, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> presented his invasion as a liberation. In 133 BCE Ionia became part of the Roman province of Asia, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> as capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From the 7th century BCE Ionia, above all Miletus, was home to the Ionian school of philosophy, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a> and his student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaximander">Anaximander</a>, which sought explanations of natural phenomena without recourse to myth. A distinct school of art flourished between 700 and 500 BCE. The Persian word Younan for Greek, the Hebrew Yavan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> yavana all derive from Ionia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ionia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He then defeated Croesus of Lydia, traditionally at the Battle of Thymbra, and captured Sardis around 547–546 BCE, bringing the <b>Ionian Greek</b> cities of the Aegean coast under Persian suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greco-Persian Wars began with the conquest of the Greek cities of <b>Ionia</b> by the Achaemenid Persian Empire of Cyrus the Great shortly after 550 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Euthydemus, an <b>Ionian Greek</b> from Magnesia according to Polybius and possibly satrap of Sogdiana, overthrew Diodotus II around 230–220 BCE; his rule reached into Sogdiana and Ferghana beyond Alexandria Eschate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greek cities of <b>Ionia</b> and Aeolis, once subdued, kept their own institutions but paid tribute and provided troops, and the Phrygians and other peoples west of the Halys acknowledged Lydian supremacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Miletus was the leading city of archaic <b>Ionia</b>: a trading power that planted dozens of colonies around the Black Sea, the home of Thales, Anaximander and Anaximenes, and the instigator of the Ionian Revolt, whose destruction by Persia in 494 BCE helped set off the Persian Wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Ionian Greeks</b> long feared them: an inscription of 283 BCE records Samos and Priene still disputing the territory of Batinētis abandoned during the invasion.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
